# Copy to server/.env and fill in. Docker Compose reads it; `npm start` in server/ does too.

# Required. The shared secret both phones type once to join your household.
# Make it long: anyone who knows it can join.
HOUSEHOLD_CODE=

# Shown in the app. The admin command `household-name` changes it without a restart, and from then
# on its name wins over this one.
HOUSEHOLD_NAME=Our home

# Optional. Turns on the AI helper ("check my Japanese", "how do I say this?").
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5

# Your home time zone. A study day ends at 4 a.m. here; it decides the shared streak.
# TZ=America/Chicago

# HTTPS: the phones need it. Browsers only let a page work offline (service worker), record voice
# and keep its storage on https:// addresses (and http://localhost). Opened as
# http://192.168.1.20:8787, Issho pairs and chats at home, but every load needs the server, voice
# messages are off, and nothing works away from home. Pick one:
#
# 1. Tailscale (recommended: real certificate, works at home and away, nothing exposed to the
#    internet). Install Tailscale on this machine and both phones, turn on MagicDNS and HTTPS
#    certificates in the Tailscale admin console, then on this machine:
#      tailscale serve --bg 8787
#    and open https://<machine-name>.<your-tailnet>.ts.net on the phones. Set TRUST_PROXY below.
#
# 2. Caddy in front of the server, with a domain name pointing at your home (Caddy gets a
#    Let's Encrypt certificate by itself; that needs ports 80 and 443 forwarded to this machine,
#    or Caddy's DNS challenge). A Caddyfile like:
#      issho.example.com {
#        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787
#      }
#    and set TRUST_PROXY below.
#    (Caddy's `tls internal` works without a domain, but each phone must install and trust
#    Caddy's root certificate first.)
#
# Then open Issho from the https:// address before pairing; the app's offline copy belongs to it.

# Behind a reverse proxy (Caddy, nginx, Tailscale serve, …) set this to the address the proxy
# connects from, so rate limits see real client IPs: an IP, a CIDR range or "loopback", several
# comma-separated. Under Docker the proxy usually arrives from Docker's networks, 172.16.0.0/12.
# Avoid "true": it also believes X-Forwarded-For entries a client makes up.
# TRUST_PROXY=172.16.0.0/12

# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Only used outside Docker; docker-compose.yml pins these inside the container.
# Relative paths are from server/.
# PORT=8787
# HOST=0.0.0.0
# DATA_DIR=./data
# STATIC_DIR=../dist
